No. On Volta or newer, `__activemask()` inside the branch is not guaranteed to include all 19 branch-taking lanes, even though the full warp started converged. It reports the lanes active at that particular instruction; it does not synchronize them. I’m interpreting “active a mask” as `__activemask()`.

The precise contracts are:
- Each caller of `__shfl_sync` must have its own lane bit set in the mask. All non-exited threads named in that mask must execute the same shuffle intrinsic with the same mask; otherwise, the behavior is undefined.
- The source lane must actively participate in that shuffle. If physical lane 18 is absent from a caller’s participating group, the retrieved value is undefined. A mask does not compact or renumber lanes: with width 32, source index 18 means physical lane 18.

Thus, even if the masks obtained inside the branch satisfy the participation contract for separate groups, a group without lane 18 cannot obtain its float reliably.

Construct the participant mask with a ballot before diverging, then have exactly lanes 0 through 18 execute the shuffle:

```cpp
__device__ float broadcast_lane18(float value)
{
    const unsigned linearThread =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned lane = linearThread % 32u;
    const bool participates = lane <= 18u;

    const unsigned mask =
        __ballot_sync(0xffffffffu, participates);

    if (participates) {
        value = __shfl_sync(mask, value, 18, 32);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Call this function from all 32 non-exited lanes of each full physical warp, with each lane supplying its own float; use block sizes that are multiples of 32 and do not call it from a partial warp. The ballot produces `0x0007ffff`, so all 19 named lanes execute the same shuffle, and lane 18 is a participating source. Every lane from 0 through 18 receives lane 18’s input float; lanes 19 through 31 never call the shuffle and retain their own values. No shared memory, atomics, or additional `__syncwarp` is needed.

Use five explicitly masked shuffle stages to build a sum in lane 0, then a sixth shuffle to broadcast it to all 19 callers. Every caller executes every shuffle; lanes without a partner read themselves and perform no addition. The explicit mask provides synchronization of participating lanes, so this does not rely on implicit lockstep execution.

Keeping the preceding function’s signature, its operation is now a sum rather than a lane-18 broadcast:

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ float broadcast_lane18(float value)
{
    const unsigned linearThread =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned lane = linearThread % 32u;
    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    for (unsigned stride = 1u; stride <= 16u; stride <<= 1u) {
        const bool hasPartner =
            (lane % (2u * stride) == 0u) &&
            (lane + stride <= 18u);
        const unsigned source = hasPartner ? lane + stride : lane;

        const float partner =
            __shfl_sync(mask, value, static_cast<int>(source), 32);

        if (hasPartner) {
            value = __fadd_rn(value, partner);
        }
    }

    return __shfl_sync(mask, value, 0, 32);
}
```

Compile with NVCC for Volta or newer, for example `nvcc -arch=sm_70 -c sum.cu`. This is a device function: invoke it exactly once from each physical lane 0 through 18 of the same full warp, and never from lanes 19 through 31. Physical lane numbering uses the block’s flattened thread index, so this also handles multidimensional blocks.

The addition order is an adjacent, left-plus-right binary tree. Writing `a[i]` for each input and `⊕` for round-to-nearest float addition, the first stage forms `a[0] ⊕ a[1]`, `a[2] ⊕ a[3]`, and so on through `a[16] ⊕ a[17]`, leaving `a[18]` alone. Subsequent stages combine adjacent groups of sizes 2, 4, 8, and 16; in particular, lane 16 forms `(a[16] ⊕ a[17]) ⊕ a[18]`, and the last reduction stage adds that result to lane 0’s sum of inputs 0 through 15. Finite inputs can still overflow during float addition; this guarantees the specified floating-point reduction, not an exact real-number sum.

For correctness, before the stage with stride s, every lane divisible by s holds the tree sum of the interval from that lane through `min(lane + s - 1, 18)`. Initially s is 1, so this is simply its own input. A lane divisible by 2s combines its interval with the disjoint adjacent interval starting at `lane + s`, if that interval exists; otherwise it keeps its value. Thus the invariant holds for groups of size 2s. After stride 16, lane 0 contains all 19 inputs, each appearing exactly once in the addition tree. The final broadcast returns that same value to every caller.

Every shuffle source is valid: a real partner is explicitly checked to be at most lane 18, and otherwise the source is the caller itself. The final source is lane 0. All these lanes are named in `0x0007ffffu`, and each caller’s own bit is also set. All 19 lanes execute the same five loop collectives and the final broadcast with the same mask and width, including lanes with no partner; only the addition is conditional. Each matching `__shfl_sync` synchronizes the named participants for that exchange, and no other lane needs to call it.

Use one shuffle to fold lanes 16–18 into lanes 0–2, retaining identical copies in lanes 16–18, then four XOR shuffle-and-add stages. That uses five float shuffles per caller, and every caller computes its own completed sum—the final operation is an addition, not a broadcast. I’m interpreting “completed some” as “completed sum.”

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ float broadcast_lane18(float value)
{
    const unsigned linearThread =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned lane = linearThread % 32u;
    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    // Physical lanes 16..18 replicate logical lanes 0..2.
    const unsigned logical = lane < 16u ? lane : lane - 16u;

    const unsigned foldSource =
        lane < 3u ? lane + 16u :
        lane >= 16u ? lane - 16u : lane;
    const float folded =
        __shfl_sync(mask, value, static_cast<int>(foldSource), 32);

    if (lane < 3u) {
        value = __fadd_rn(value, folded);
    } else if (lane >= 16u) {
        value = __fadd_rn(folded, value);
    }

    for (unsigned stride = 1u; stride <= 8u; stride <<= 1u) {
        const unsigned source = logical ^ stride;
        const float partner =
            __shfl_sync(mask, value, static_cast<int>(source), 32);
        value = __fadd_rn(value, partner);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Compile this device code with `nvcc -arch=sm_70 -c file.cu`, or target a newer architecture. Its calling contract is exactly the stated one: physical lanes 0–18 of a full warp each call once, and lanes 19–31 do not call. The signature is preserved, although the function now returns a sum rather than broadcasting lane 18.

Addition order: write the original inputs as x0 through x18. The fold constructs A0 = RN(x0 + x16), A1 = RN(x1 + x17), A2 = RN(x2 + x18), and Aj = xj for j = 3 through 15. Physical lanes 16–18 hold identical copies of A0–A2, using the same operand order. Then, simultaneously at each stride s = 1, 2, 4, 8, logical lane j updates its accumulator to RN(its previous accumulator + logical lane j XOR s’s previous accumulator). Here RN is the round-to-nearest float addition explicitly requested by `__fadd_rn`. Different callers can have different rounded results because their operand orders differ; each result is a reduction of all 19 inputs. Finite inputs do not preclude overflow during those additions.

Exact-once coverage: initially, the 16 logical groups are {0,16}, {1,17}, {2,18}, and the singleton groups {3} through {15}. These groups partition all 19 inputs. After k XOR stages, logical lane j contains precisely the groups whose indices agree with j above the lowest k bits. The next XOR partner differs in bit k, so its group set is disjoint and supplies exactly the missing half of the enlarged set. After four stages, every logical lane contains all 16 groups, hence all 19 inputs exactly once. The replicated physical lanes maintain the same accumulator as their corresponding logical lanes by induction: they begin identically, read the same partner, and perform the same ordered addition.

Source validity: the fold reads lanes 16–18, lanes 0–2, or the caller itself, all within 0–18. Every subsequent source is `logical XOR stride`, which is within 0–15. Thus every source participates.

Matching collectives: all 19 callers unconditionally execute the fold shuffle and the same four loop shuffles, with the identical mask and width. Each caller’s bit is set in the mask, and every named lane executes each matching collective. Lanes without a fold partner still execute that shuffle using themselves as the source; they simply skip the addition. The `_sync` collectives provide the required rendezvous, so the implementation does not depend on implicit lockstep execution.

Only negative cycles reachable from S and able to reach T make this query unbounded. I’m interpreting “classify underscore root” as `classify_root(N, edges, S, T)`. Use reverse reachability to exclude vertices that cannot reach T, then Bellman–Ford to distinguish a finite minimum from an unbounded walk cost.

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import sys


def classify_root(N, edges, S, T):
    edges = list(edges)
    reverse = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    for u, v, w in edges:
        reverse[v].append(u)

    # Mark precisely the vertices from which T is reachable.
    reaches_t = [False] * N
    reaches_t[T] = True
    stack = [T]
    while stack:
        v = stack.pop()
        for u in reverse[v]:
            if not reaches_t[u]:
                reaches_t[u] = True
                stack.append(u)

    if not reaches_t[S]:
        return ('unreachable', None)

    relevant_edges = [
        (u, v, w) for u, v, w in edges
        if reaches_t[u] and reaches_t[v]
    ]
    K = sum(reaches_t)
    dist = [None] * N
    dist[S] = 0

    # Synchronous rounds: each round adds at most one edge.
    for _ in range(K - 1):
        next_dist = dist.copy()
        changed = False
        for u, v, w in relevant_edges:
            if dist[u] is None:
                continue
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if next_dist[v] is None or candidate < next_dist[v]:
                next_dist[v] = candidate
                changed = True
        dist = next_dist
        if not changed:
            return ('finite', dist[T])

    # A further improvement certifies a relevant negative cycle.
    for u, v, w in relevant_edges:
        if dist[u] is not None:
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if dist[v] is None or candidate < dist[v]:
                return ('unbounded', None)

    return ('finite', dist[T])


def main():
    # Input: N M S T, followed by M triples u v weight.
    tokens = iter(map(int, sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()))
    N = next(tokens)
    M = next(tokens)
    S = next(tokens)
    T = next(tokens)
    edges = [(next(tokens), next(tokens), next(tokens))
             for _ in range(M)]
    status, cost = classify_root(N, edges, S, T)
    print(status, cost if cost is not None else 'None')


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
```

Run with Python 3, for example `python3 solution.py < input.txt`. The function returns exactly the requested pair; the command-line wrapper prints its two components.

**Invariant and correctness.** After round k, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of any S-to-v walk using at most k edges in the retained graph, or `None` if no such walk exists. Initially only the zero-edge walk at S exists. Copying the previous distances preserves shorter walks, and relaxing from the previous array considers exactly all walks formed by adding one edge, proving the invariant inductively.

Filtering is safe: every vertex on an S-to-T walk can reach T, so no candidate walk is removed. If S fails the reverse-reachability test, no S-to-T walk exists.

If there is no reachable negative cycle in the retained graph, cycles in a walk can be removed without increasing its cost. Thus a minimum has a simple representative with at most K−1 edges, and the invariant gives its cost. Conversely, an improvement after K−1 rounds implies a reachable negative cycle: without one, all reachable vertices would already have their simple-path minima. Every retained vertex can reach T, so that cycle can be reached from S, repeated arbitrarily many times, and followed by a path to T; its negative cost makes the answer unbounded. A relevant reachable negative cycle also cannot escape detection: if no edge improved, all its edge inequalities could be summed to imply that its total weight is nonnegative, a contradiction.

Early termination is safe because an unchanged round is a fixed point of the relaxation operation. Parallel edges and self-loops are processed individually. When S equals T, the empty walk gives cost zero unless a negative cycle is reachable from S and can return to S.

**Worst-case bounds.** With M edges, time is O(N(N+M)), including distance-array copies, and auxiliary memory is O(N+M). Here there are at most 2,000 rounds including detection and at most 20 million edge examinations. Integer magnitudes stay within about N times the maximum absolute weight—at most 2 × 10^15—because synchronous rounds construct walks of at most N edges; Python integers represent these exactly.

A negative cycle makes the answer unbounded only if it is reachable from S and can reach T. The code below enforces the second condition by reverse reachability and the first by relaxing only from a known, finite distance; it uses deterministic Bellman–Ford rounds, not a queue-based heuristic. I’m interpreting “classify underscore root” as `classify_root(N, edges, S, T)`.

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import sys


def classify_root(N, edges, S, T):
    edges = list(edges)
    reverse = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    for u, v, w in edges:
        reverse[v].append(u)

    # Identify all vertices that can reach T.
    reaches_t = [False] * N
    reaches_t[T] = True
    stack = [T]
    while stack:
        v = stack.pop()
        for u in reverse[v]:
            if not reaches_t[u]:
                reaches_t[u] = True
                stack.append(u)

    if not reaches_t[S]:
        return ('unreachable', None)

    relevant_edges = [
        (u, v, w) for u, v, w in edges
        if reaches_t[u] and reaches_t[v]
    ]
    K = sum(reaches_t)
    dist = [None] * N
    dist[S] = 0

    # Synchronous rounds: candidates use only the previous round.
    for _ in range(K - 1):
        next_dist = dist.copy()
        changed = False
        for u, v, w in relevant_edges:
            if dist[u] is None:
                continue
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if next_dist[v] is None or candidate < next_dist[v]:
                next_dist[v] = candidate
                changed = True
        dist = next_dist
        if not changed:
            return ('finite', dist[T])

    # Test for a strict improvement in round K, without mutation.
    for u, v, w in relevant_edges:
        if dist[u] is None:
            continue
        candidate = dist[u] + w
        if dist[v] is None or candidate < dist[v]:
            return ('unbounded', None)

    return ('finite', dist[T])


def main():
    # Input: N M S T, followed by M triples: u v weight.
    tokens = iter(map(int, sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()))
    N = next(tokens)
    M = next(tokens)
    S = next(tokens)
    T = next(tokens)
    edges = [(next(tokens), next(tokens), next(tokens))
             for _ in range(M)]
    status, cost = classify_root(N, edges, S, T)
    print(status, cost if cost is not None else 'None')


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
```

Run with `python3 solution.py < input.txt`; vertices are numbered 0 through N−1.

**Invariant.** After r synchronous rounds, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of an S-to-v walk using at most r edges in the retained graph, or `None` if no such walk exists. Initially only the empty walk at S exists. Copying the old distances preserves shorter walks, and relaxing from the old array extends a walk by exactly one edge, establishing the invariant inductively. The `if dist[u] is None: continue` guard prevents an unreachable distance from ever participating in arithmetic.

**Exactly what establishes unboundedness.** After K−1 rounds, the final scan finds an edge `(u, v, w)` with reachable u and `dist[u] + w < dist[v]`. The code also checks `dist[v] is None`, although that case cannot occur here: any reachable vertex in this K-vertex graph has a simple path of at most K−1 edges. Thus the witness is a strict improvement achievable with at most K edges beyond every walk of at most K−1 edges.

Such an improving walk must contain a negative cycle. Otherwise, removing its repeated-vertex cycles would yield a simple path with no greater cost and at most K−1 edges, contradicting the improvement. That cycle is reachable from S, and every retained vertex can reach T, so repeating the cycle produces arbitrarily negative S-to-T walk costs.

Conversely, if a reachable negative cycle exists in the retained graph, all its vertices have finite distances after K−1 rounds. If no edge of that cycle were relaxable, summing the inequalities `dist[v] <= dist[u] + w` around it would imply its total weight is nonnegative—a contradiction. Therefore the final scan detects every relevant negative cycle. Without one, removing nonnegative cycles leaves a minimum-cost simple path, whose cost is already represented by `dist[T]`. An unchanged earlier round is a fixed point, so its early return is also safe.

**Boundary cases.** An unreachable T returns `('unreachable', None)` before relaxation. When S equals T, the empty walk supplies cost zero: the result is `('finite', 0)` unless a negative cycle is reachable from S and can return to S, in which case it is unbounded. Negative self-loops and parallel edges require no special handling.

**Worst-case bounds.** Let M be the number of edges and M′ the number retained. Reachability and filtering take O(N+M). There are at most K−1 rounds, each copying N distances and scanning M′ edges, followed by one final scan: total time is O(N+M+K(N+M′)), hence O(N(N+M)) for every allowed input. Auxiliary memory is O(N+M). This bound is deterministic, includes distance-array copies, and makes no assumption about early termination. Python integers are exact; candidates use at most K edges, so their magnitudes are at most K times 10¹² under the supplied limits.

Here’s a complete C++17 FIFO with exactly 17 slots: push rejects overflow, and pop returns false when empty without changing its output argument. The head and tail wrap modulo 17, while a separate count distinguishes full from empty; this version stores integers and is not thread-safe. The test covers empty reads, full capacity, rejected overflow, FIFO draining, and wraparound with live elements.

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <cassert>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

class BoundedFIFO {
public:
    static constexpr std::size_t capacity = 17;

    bool push(int value) noexcept {
        if (count_ == capacity) {
            return false;
        }
        data_[tail_] = value;
        tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % capacity;
        ++count_;
        return true;
    }

    bool pop(int& value) noexcept {
        if (count_ == 0) {
            return false;
        }
        value = data_[head_];
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % capacity;
        --count_;
        return true;
    }

    std::size_t size() const noexcept { return count_; }
    bool empty() const noexcept {
